    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1217">

                <pb facs="1203" n="1037" sameAs="frus:frus1917/pg1037"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.115R39/57</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Chargé Summerlin </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 404</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-12-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 26, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s telegrams
                    Nos. 620 and 627, dated, respectively, December 8, 4 p.m., and December 12, 4
                    p.m., <pb facs="1204" n="1038" xml:id="pg1038"/> 1917, on the subject of the
                    attack on Esperanza and the burning of the headquarters of the Richardson
                    Construction Company by Yaqui Indians, I enclose herewith copies of a despatch
                    and of its enclosure from the American Consul at Guaymas, which give an account
                    of conditions in the Yaqui Valley, and of the attack, by Yaqui Indians, on
                    Esperanza and the burning of the Richardson Company’s headquarters.</p>
                <p>You will endeavor to impress upon the Foreign Office the seriousness of the
                    situation in the Yaqui Valley as affecting American lives and property.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
